cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software, cite it as below. If you cite a measured figure, cite the commit and the split it came from."
title: "Project ELIZA: a forensic investigation suite with a disclose-not-distribute gate"
version: 0.1.0
date-released: "2026-11-01"
license: CDDL-1.1
type: software
abstract: >-
  A forensic investigation suite: it keeps an investigation's claims tied to the
  documents they rest on, and it refuses to publish the evidence those claims
  came from. The centre of the design is an allowlist rather than a deny-list --
  nothing ships that PUBLIC_MANIFEST.json does not name -- so a new evidence
  directory cannot reach the public artifact by being forgotten about; the
  release gate reads the same manifest and fails the build when a path crosses
  the line. Material that stays home is enumerated, not implied: raw primary
  documents, entity-resolution output, rendered findings, and correspondence
  naming identified organisations and people. A reader verifies a claim by
  re-fetching the source and comparing its SHA-256 rather than receiving the
  case, which is why the instruments run against data the reader supplies. This
  is the first project created and landed in the tree, and it is NOT the
  therapeutic agent -- that is a separate project, Project Nurse Ratchet.
  Named for Weizenbaum's ELIZA (1966): a system believed to be doing work that
  nothing underneath was doing.
authors:
  - name: "Project ELIZA contributors"
keywords:
  - forensic investigation
  - evidence provenance
  - disclosure control
  - allowlist release gate
  - claim-to-source citation
  - reproducible verification
  - open science
references:
  - type: article
    title: "ELIZA - a computer program for the study of natural language communication between man and machine"
    authors:
      - family-names: Weizenbaum
    journal: "Communications of the ACM"
    year: 1966
    notes: "The cautionary tale this project is named after. No code from it is included."
# The analytical instruments this suite carries implement the psychometric and
# decision-model framing already cited in full by the sibling therapeutic
# project (Project Nurse Ratchet, CITATION.cff). They are not re-cited here,
# because a forensic citation that pointed at psychometrics would be describing
# the wrong project -- the error this file previously contained.
# repository-code, url and the author's identity are added at publication.
# They are deliberately absent rather than guessed: a citation file that points
# at a URL nobody has reserved is worse than one that says it will be added.
